 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 53,
   "y": 9,
   "text": "THE FALKIRK WHEEL. Opened in 2002, the only rotating boat lift in the world. It lifts boats 24 metres between two canals in about five minutes. It used to take eleven locks and a free afternoon."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "FORTH AND CLYDE CANAL. Opened in 1790 to link the east and west coasts of Scotland, so ships needn't go all the way round the top. The towpath is for walkers, cyclists and the occasional heron who thinks it owns the right of way."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 10,
   "text": "THE UNION CANAL. About thirty-one miles with no locks at all: it runs on one level, which is why it's called a contour canal. It brought coal into Edinburgh. Now it brings in rowers and dog walkers."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "THE KELPIES. Two steel horse heads, thirty metres tall, honouring the heavy horses that once pulled barges and ploughs, and named for the water spirit of legend. Bigger than the legend, and better behaved."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "LINLITHGOW PALACE. A royal palace ruined by fire, with a loch at its door. Mary, Queen of Scots, was born here in 1542, which is a lot to live up to as a first day."
  },
  {
   "x": 56,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "THE ANTONINE WALL. Nearby ran a Roman wall of turf, built around AD 142 on a stone base. It was the northern edge of the Roman Empire, for about twenty years. The Romans left. The turf, mostly, stayed."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 29,
   "plaque": "central_belt_bannockburn",
   "title": "Bannockburn",
   "text": "Not far north, near Stirling, on 23 and 24 June 1314, Robert the Bruce's army beat the much larger English army of Edward II at Bannockburn. It secured Bruce's crown, though the war rumbled on for years. A famous statue of Bruce on his horse now looks out over the field."
  },
  {
   "x": 59,
   "y": 20,
   "plaque": "central_belt_forth_bridge",
   "title": "The Forth Bridge",
   "text": "East of here the Forth Bridge carries the railway across the Firth of Forth on three huge red cantilevers. It opened in 1890, a marvel of Victorian steel, and became a World Heritage Site in 2015. Around six and a half million rivets hold it together. Nobody has counted them twice."
  }
 ],
